PhD Student:  ```DIALOGUE
Hi, thanks for your outline—it has some compelling components! I have an idea for an experiment that builds on our literature insights but takes a slightly different approach. Imagine designing a modular LLM-based system that “learns to label” labor market shifts around key AI product releases. Here’s my proposal:

1. We start by focusing on one well-documented event (e.g., the ChatGPT launch in November 2022) and collect open, license-clean data from the OII Online Labour Index, complemented by economic indicators from the World Bank. This will serve as our temporal backbone.

2. Using a pre-trained GPT model, we develop a dynamic prompt module that is fine-tuned via reinforcement learning specifically on qualitative segments from historical labor market reports. The RL framework will iteratively adjust prompts—our “trainable units” (as inspired by PromptFlow)—to better capture signals of labor displacement (P1), shared prosperity (P3), and detectability (P6). The module’s objective is to generate classification labels for segments of the timeline indicating potential shocks and structural shifts.

3. For the taxonomy mapping, we implement an automated mapping tool that uses LLM-based qualitative coding. It will translate narrative job descriptions pulled from online job platforms into our six measurable propositions (A–F). This mapping will not only work as a labeling mechanism but also serve to evaluate the socio-economic narratives, aligning them with the designated policy simulation scenarios.

4. For evaluation, we would deploy a staggered-adoption Difference-in-Differences approach, focusing on the weeks immediately before and after the ChatGPT release, with robustness checks (anchored on alternative events like Stable Diffusion’s launch) for validating our measures. Evaluation modules would be pre-registered with clear definitions of primary and secondary outcomes for each proposition.

5. Finally, to ensure our simulation reflects ethical practices, we’ll limit our data collection to open APIs and archives (e.g., OpenAlex, Internet Archive) and review all procedures with our IRB guidelines.

This simple experiment, centering on a well-defined event and using an LLM-based dynamic prompt tuning system, could serve as a proof-of-concept for measuring AI’s impacts on labor. It leverages reinforcement learning to continuously refine prompt efficacy and a transparent taxonomy mapping that translates qualitative job data into quantifiable signals.

What do you think about this iterative, self-improving system for simulating labor market shifts? Any additional thoughts on refining the prompt adaptation process or the data collection window? Looking forward to your feedback!
``` 
~~~~~~~~~~~
