Postdoc:  ```PLAN
Title: ChatGPT Event Labor Impact Simulation via Two-Stage Dynamic Prompt Tuning

Overview:
We propose a proof-of-concept experiment to simulate and measure the impact of the ChatGPT release (November 2022) on the labor market. Our approach combines a two-stage dynamic prompt adaptation system with a lightweight LLM-based qualitative classifier, operationalizing labor impact propositions (A–F) in a compact, transparent experiment.

1. Data Curation:
   • Focus exclusively on the ChatGPT release event.
   • Collect timeline data from open sources: the OII Online Labour Index and World Bank economic indicators.
   • Supplement with license-clean job narratives from open occupational datasets (e.g., OpenAlex, Internet Archive) to capture qualitative job descriptions.
   • Define a strict analysis window: 4 weeks before and 4 weeks after the event.

2. Two-Stage Dynamic Prompt Tuning:
   Stage 1 – Initialization:
     • Curate a small dataset of labor market segments directly associated with the ChatGPT event.
     • Fine-tune a pre-trained GPT model (e.g., GPT-3 or similar) using human-validated prompts that spotlight key impact signals: labor displacement (P1), shifts in bargaining power/shared prosperity (P3), and detectability (P6).
   Stage 2 – Adaptive Update:
     • Implement a gradient-based meta-learning update to iteratively adjust prompt formulations as weekly new data arrives.
     • Aim for swift adaptation to subtle temporal labor market changes, ensuring our model’s sensitivity in capturing emerging signals.

3. LLM-Based Qualitative Classifier for Taxonomy Mapping:
   • Develop a lightweight classifier that leverages the fine-tuned GPT model to translate open-text job narratives into quantifiable labels corresponding to our six propositions:
       A) Labor-market shocks and technical-debt externalities,
       B) Pace/distributional effects of automation,
       C) Shared prosperity and bargaining power,
       D) Uneven democratization across countries,
       E) Learning harms and content homogenization,
       F) Detectability/watermarking and copyright compensation.
   • Implement iterative updates to the classifier using incoming weekly labeled segments.

4. Evaluation Framework:
   • Use a staggered-adoption Difference-in-Differences (DiD) model focused solely on the ChatGPT event.
   • Pre-register a concise analysis plan delineating:
       - Primary outcomes: Quantitative labor displacement signals (e.g., changes in employment metrics, rapid shifts in job posting narratives).
       - Secondary outcomes: Indicators for shared prosperity, detectability, and related propositions.
   • Incorporate robustness checks by comparing with a secondary event (e.g., a brief window around the Stable Diffusion launch) if time permits.

5. Ethical and Transparent Research Practices:
   • Rely exclusively on open APIs and archival sources to ensure data licensing compliance; no web scraping of proprietary data.
   • Follow regulatory and IRB guidelines rigorously to protect privacy and ensure data anonymization.
   • Document all assumptions, potential confounders (e.g., seasonal trends, macro shocks), and model limitations, including risks of false positives in detection.

Outcomes:
This experiment aims to provide a clear, simple demonstration of how dynamic prompt engineering—via a two-stage adaptation process—and a lightweight classification system can accurately simulate labor market shifts. By focusing on one well-demarcated event, we seek to operationalize key socio-economic propositions and establish a scalable protocol for broader AI impact simulations in the future.
  
Next steps include finalizing the human-curated dataset, pre-registering the analysis plan, and setting up the iterative model training pipelines.
